{"causes": ["The disillusionment following the devastation of World War I prompted a widespread rejection of traditional values and rational thought.", "The intellectual and artistic precedents set by the Dada movement, particularly its anti-art stance and embrace of absurdity, provided a fertile ground for Surrealism's emergence.", "Sigmund Freud's theories of the unconscious mind, dreams, and psychoanalysis offered a scientific framework for exploring irrationality and inner desires, profoundly influencing Surrealist ideology."], "effects": ["Surrealism revolutionized artistic and literary expression, introducing new techniques like automatism and dream imagery that profoundly influenced subsequent avant-garde movements.", "The movement's exploration of the subconscious mind significantly impacted psychology, philosophy, and cultural theory, fostering new perspectives on human desire and perception.", "Surrealism's aesthetic and conceptual innovations permeated various aspects of popular culture, including advertising, fashion, and film, shaping modern visual language."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "dadaism", "sourceName": "Dadaism", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "surrealism", "targetName": "surrealism", "context": "Dadaism's anti-establishment spirit and experimental approach directly influenced the early development and rebellious ethos of Surrealism in the 1920s."}, {"sourceSlug": "sigmund-freud", "sourceName": "Sigmund Freud", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "surrealism", "targetName": "surrealism", "context": "Sigmund Freud's theories on the unconscious and dream interpretation provided a core intellectual foundation and methodology for the Surrealist movement's exploration of inner worlds in the 1920s."}, {"sourceSlug": "andre-breton", "sourceName": "André Breton", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "surrealism", "targetName": "surrealism", "context": "André Breton, through his 1924 "Manifesto of Surrealism" and subsequent leadership, was instrumental in founding and defining the theoretical framework of the Surrealist movement."}, {"sourceSlug": "surrealism", "sourceName": "surrealism", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "paris-france", "targetName": "Paris, France", "context": "Surrealism was formally launched and primarily developed within the artistic and intellectual circles of Paris, France, during the 1920s and 1930s."}, {"sourceSlug": "surrealism", "sourceName": "surrealism", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "salvador-dali", "targetName": "Salvador Dalí", "context": "Surrealism profoundly influenced the artistic style and thematic concerns of Salvador Dalí, leading him to create some of his most iconic dreamscapes and paranoiac-critical works from the late 1920s."}], "places": [{"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Exile hub"}, {"name": "Mexico City, Mexico", "role": "Artistic refuge"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: surrealism", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "surrealism"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q39427", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surrealism"], "tags": ["art style", "Modernism & Avant-Garde", "aesthetic-theory"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "surrealism left a notable mark on Modern history, documented across 117 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced intellectual traditions and cultural development.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 8, "significanceNarrative": "Surrealism fundamentally reshaped 20th-century art, literature, and thought, influencing subsequent avant-garde movements and popular culture globally. Its radical exploration of the subconscious and challenge to rationalism left an indelible mark on how society perceives reality and artistic expression.", "significanceCategory": "continental"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-26T07:41:39Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28223835253", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "International cultural movement that began in the early 1920s Originating c. 1920 CE.", "newValue": "Surrealism emerged as a revolutionary international cultural movement in Paris around 1924, fundamentally challenging rationalism and bourgeois conventions by prioritizing the unconscious mind. Spearheaded by André Breton, who authored the foundational "Manifesto of Surrealism," the movement sought to liberate human imagination and desire, believing that the dream state and automatism held deeper truths than waking reality. Its core figures, including Salvador Dalí, René Magritte, Max Ernst, and Joan Miró, explored diverse media, from painting and sculpture to literature, film, and photography, to express these inner worlds, profoundly altering the trajectory of modern art and thought.

Initially rooted in the anti-establishment spirit of Dada, Surrealism quickly developed its distinct methods, such as automatic writing, frottage, and exquisite corpse, to bypass conscious control and tap into the subconscious. The movement's first major exhibition took place in Paris in 1925, showcasing works that juxtaposed disparate objects and dreamlike imagery, aiming to shock and provoke. Key literary contributions, like Breton's "Nadja" (1928), explored the intersection of everyday life and the marvelous, while cinematic works by Luis Buñuel and Dalí, such as "Un Chien Andalou" (1929), pushed the boundaries of narrative and visual logic.

Surrealism's legacy extends far beyond the art world, permeating advertising, fashion, film, and popular culture throughout the 20th century and into the 21st. Its radical questioning of societal norms and its embrace of the irrational paved the way for subsequent artistic movements, including Abstract Expressionism and Pop Art, by legitimizing non-representational and conceptual approaches. The movement also exerted significant influence on psychoanalytic theory and philosophy, inspiring thinkers to further explore the hidden dimensions of human experience and desire."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-26T07:41:39Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28223835253", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Earlier philosophical traditions in the 1800 – 1945 CE", "type": "Idea", "year": "1800"}, {"title": "Empirical observations in the 1800 – 1945 CE", "type": "Idea", "year": "1800"}, {"title": "Cultural context in the 1800 – 1945 CE", "type": "Idea", "year": "1800"}], "newValue": ["The disillusionment following the devastation of World War I prompted a widespread rejection of traditional values and rational thought.", "The intellectual and artistic precedents set by the Dada movement, particularly its anti-art stance and embrace of absurdity, provided a fertile ground for Surrealism's emergence.", "Sigmund Freud's theories of the unconscious mind, dreams, and psychoanalysis offered a scientific framework for exploring irrationality and inner desires, profoundly influencing Surrealist ideology."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-26T07:41:39Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28223835253", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Influenced later thought through surrealism's historical significance", "type": "Idea"}, {"title": "Shaped institutions through surrealism's historical significance", "type": "Idea"}, {"title": "Transformed practice through surrealism's historical significance", "type": "Idea"}], "newValue": ["Surrealism revolutionized 